    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70618,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Testis, paratestis, and spermatic cord</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:34:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70619,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients usually present with a painless enlargement of the testis. Occasionally, pain or heaviness is reported. Tumours can also be an incidental finding {12445137; 15737039; 26600891}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:34:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70620,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mucinous cystadenoma is extremely rare in the testis and paratestis. The few reported cases have occurred in patients over a wide age range (11&ndash;78&nbsp;years) {12960806; 23044077}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:34:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70621,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The histogenesis is debated; proposed hypotheses include an origin from a M&uuml;llerian remnant or M&uuml;llerian metaplasia of the mesothelium.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:34:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70622,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:34:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70623,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The reported cases are unilateral cystic masses, usually unilocular or occasionally multilocular, containing mucoid or watery fluid {23044077; 15201756}. The tumours are well demarcated and have a fibrotic wall. Reported tumour sizes range from 10 to 90&nbsp;mm {12960806; 12445137}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:34:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70624,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "<p>The cystic cavities are lined by a single layer of columnar epithelium that resembles intestinal, gastric, or cervical mucosa, without the cellular atypia and architectural complexity seen in their ovarian counterparts {28189139; 12960806}. Scattered goblet cells are often present in the intestinal epithelium {28189139}. No stromal invasion or necrosis is seen. Mucin extravasation and a mixed inflammatory infiltrate of lymphocytes, plasma cells, and histiocytes can be identified {28189139}.</p>\r\n<p>The few reported cases with immunohistochemistry analysis showed CK20 and CDX2 positivity in the columnar cells, with variable expression of CK7. Scattered chromogranin-positive neuroendocrine cells can be identified within the epithelium. The goblet cells are MUC2-positive {15737039; 28189139}.</p>\r\n<p>Primary mucinous cystadenoma needs to be distinguished from mucinous differentiation in a testicular germ cell tumour. Teratomatous elements should be absent. Postpubertal teratomas typically show gain of chromosome&nbsp;12p. Therefore, an absence of 12p&nbsp;chromosomal gain on molecular testing and a lack of background germ cell neoplasia in&nbsp;situ can be used to exclude mucinous differentiation in postpubertal teratomas {27796764; 23598964}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-04-15T17:42:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70625,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:34:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
